{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/tests/support/stored_graph.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Domain requires this callback even for a storage-only consumer. The\n delegation test must use only node_path; discovering edges would be\n an unwanted graph access, which this guard rejects. Traversal fixtures\n separately exercise edge discovery."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/support/stored_graph.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is acceptable.\n\n- **Source agrees with the commentary.** The function body only panics with \"storage operations must not discover graph edges\". The commentary says this is a guard that fails if edges are discovered.\n- **The delegation test fits the claim.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/storage.rs#fn[consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path]` uses `StoredGraph` only through `load_evidence`, `store_evidence` and `node_path`. It never calls `outgoing_edges`, so the guard is not meant to run there.\n- **The other `StoredGraph` test does not run it either.** `storage.rs#fn[malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors]` uses only storage operations and `Validator::verify` on a single-node criterion. `tests/outline_content.rs` only names the `ValidationCache` type for `StoredGraph`.\n- **Edge discovery is exercised elsewhere.** `tests/contracts.rs` has `Application`/`Relationships` `Domain` fixtures whose `outgoing_edges` are called and asserted on. This supports \"Traversal fixtures separately exercise edge discovery.\"\n- **The reason is substantive.** The `Domain` trait requires the callback, and the intended behavior is for it never to run, so a zero-execution function here is deliberate and not merely hard to cover.\n\nThe citation is on an explicit function with a body, which is a permitted use. I found no gap.",
